  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThis strategy employs a gradient-based optimization method to minimize the maximum overlap integral for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. By iteratively adjusting the function h in the direction that reduces the worst-case overlap, we aim to achieve a tighter bound on C\u2085. The approach leverages the differentiability of the overlap function with respect to h and systematically reduces the maximum overlap through projected gradient descent.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nWe represent h as a step function with n_points samples over [0, 2], where h_i \u2208 [0, 1] and \u03a3h_i = 1. The overlap for shift k is defined as:\n\nO_k = \u03a3_{i=0}^{n_points-1} h_i * (1 - h_{(i + k) mod n_points}) * dx\n\nOur goal is to minimize the maximum O_k over all possible k. The gradient of O_k with respect to h_i is computed as:\n\ndO_k/dh_i = (1 - h_{(i + k) mod n_points}) - h_{(i - k) mod n_points}\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na. Initialization:\n   - Use initial_h_values if available; otherwise, start with a uniform distribution scaled to meet the sum constraint.\n\nb. Compute overlaps:\n   - For each shift k, compute O_k using the current h.\n\nc. Find worst-case shift:\n   - Identify k_max where O_k is maximum.\n\nd. Compute gradient:\n   - Calculate dO_k_max/dh_i for each i.\n\ne. Update step:\n   - Adjust h in the direction of the negative gradient, ensuring 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 and \u03a3h_i = n_points/2.\n\nf. Repeat until convergence or budget exhaustion.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n- Initialize with multiple starting points (e.g., uniform, two-interval, random) to explore diverse configurations.\n- Use adaptive step sizes or line search to optimize convergence.\n- Apply periodic constraint projection after each update.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nThe method runs efficiently within 600 seconds, performing gradient computations and updates iteratively. It includes checks for numerical stability and ensures feasible solutions at each step.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nEnsure h satisfies constraints and evaluate the maximum overlap using the provided function. Return the best h, its associated C\u2085, and n_points.",
